💡 severity: SUGGESTION —
💡 **SUGGESTION: The REG twin duplicates ~45 lines of the AC2-paced fixture**

This new test copies the AC2-paced fixture verbatim — `Socket.connect` + `addTearDown(socket.destroy)`, the `socket.done` swallow, the read-side `listen` with the `'413'`-contains completer, the raw HTTP headers block, the 32 KiB `Timer.periodic` pump — differing only in comments and the closing 100 ms grace delay.

A small local helper would keep the twins in lockstep so a future change to one (e.g. a new header, a different chunk size) cannot silently diverge from the other:

```dart
Future<(Socket, Timer, Completer<String>)> pumpOverCapUpload(LocalHub hub) async {
  final socket = await Socket.connect('127.0.0.1', port);
  addTearDown(socket.destroy);
  unawaited(socket.done.then<void>((_) {}, onError: (Object _) {}));
  final answer = Completer<String>();
  socket.listen(/* shared 413/closed/error plumbing */);
  socket.write('POST /relay HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: ...\r\n...\r\n\r\n');
  final pump = Timer.periodic(/* 20 ms × 32 KiB */);
  addTearDown(pump.cancel);
  return (socket, pump, answer);
}
```

Both tests then read as scenario-specific one-liners (AC2-paced: answer-or-close within 1 s; REG: +100 ms post-RST grace), and the "twin" relationship becomes structural instead of copy-paste. Non-blocking polish — the duplication is clearly intentional ("twin") and each copy is fully commented.

